A||||12||mshill||FieroStore and underdrive pulley...||||9||
Z||000000||mshill||12-12-2000||05:26 PM||||I just got my FieroStore underdrive pulley in the mail yesterday...  I must say they are pretty fast, but alas, not all is well.  It seems that the pulley is so small that you cannot use the stock pulley bolts as the corners of the bolt heads cannot turn past the inside of the pulley wall.  So they ship you some hex driver bolts with round heads.  This is where it gets good.  They sent me two bolts (it obviously requires four), but not only that,  the bolts were at least 1/2 inch too long (would scre in all the way).  <P>I had to go to the local hardware store, whose metric stock is somewhat limited.  I had to pay $2.01 each for the right bolts cause they only had stainless.  So I am sending the two bolts back to the Fiero Store with a copy of my receipt so they can reimburse me the eight plus dollars that it cost me to get the right stuff.<P>I just had to whine a little bit, cause this seems to always happen to me when I buy stuff mail order.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||Raydar||12-12-2000||05:48 PM||||That's pretty sloppy of them.<BR>I bought a set of CarboMet brake pads from them, a while back. The wear sensors stuck out waaaaayyyyy too far on the caliper side of the pad. Couldn't even get them into the caliper. Had to break off nearly 3/4 inch, and re-bend the tab. Not a show stopper. Didn't cost me anything other than a few minutes time. <BR>But sloppy, nevertheless. <P>Raydar ||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||12-12-2000||05:49 PM||||   I too thought that the FieroStore was fast at shipping, but I would have rather waited than to see the package.  I ordered the set of 8mm plug wires, and inner dew strips, emblems, center caps, and such.  Well, because of how long the dewstrips were, they packaged it in a long triangle box, and becuase the boxes of some of the other stuff wouldnt fit in, the opended the boxes and packaged the contents and the torn up boxes.  I wasnt impressed.<P>   Anyway, that's pretty steep for those bolts, even in stainless.  They must have had a huge markup on those.  At my hardware store, the same ones prolly would have gone for around $.90 a piece, and that's still pretty steep when you figure in how much it actually costs to make them (about $.30), but then again, this is in a different part of the country.<P>Anyway, hope they reimburse you!<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||G-Nasty||03-06-2001||12:59 AM||||I know a larger diameter pulley would lower your rpm time. It is suppossed to be "easier" for your engines crank assembly to rotate just like a bicycles crank. The larger the crank or pulley - the  faster you would be able to bring up your RPM's. Just look at any SVT or other high performance catalog. They always have a pulley that is LARGER than the factory one. NOT SMALLER.<BR>There are sometimes issues with the charging system.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnnyK||03-06-2001||01:13 AM||||What would it matter how big the pulley is? All it is turning is accessories? You sure your not talking about gearing, or am I missing something?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||03-06-2001||12:52 PM||||A larger diameter crank pulley will make the accesories turn faster, and require more power from the engine to turn them.  Just like on a bike, if you shift to the big ring in front.  Gets pretty damn hard to move at low speed but you'll be able to go faster.<P>A small crank pulley makes it easier for the engine to turn the accesories.  It'll slow them down, but if you spend alot of time at high RPM's that's what you want.<P>Oh yeah, JohnnyK..<BR>The accesories produce what's called parasitic drag.  They consume X horsepower to run them.  It's not too bad on the Fiero, since most of the time you're just running the alternator and the waterpump.  So when you switch to a smaller pulley you make it easier for the engine to turn them and you get a faster revving engine and free up a few horsepower.  Not a whole lot, but it's there.<P>Let me repeat... a <I>larger</I> crank pulley will make it <B>harder</B> for your engine to rotate and will make it rev <B><I>slower</B></I>.  Which is why you want a smaller crank pulley.  Of course, this is also assuming that all the other pulleys in the system stay the same size.<p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 03-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||StuGood||03-06-2001||01:53 PM||||Thanks, Standard.  That's what makes sense to me, but I didn't want to have to be the one to say it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000007||JohnnyK||03-06-2001||02:18 PM||||Make you go faster? I don't understand how running the water pump and alternator faster will make your car faster? Wouldn't it just produce more power from the alternator and faster coolant flow? (Well, i know it would, I just want to hear your take on this story.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The pullet has nothing to do with the speed of the car. All it does is drive the accessories.||||reg||1
Z||000008||TheFiero64||03-06-2001||02:28 PM||||I always thought the Fiero Store was reasonable on some things, but they just peeeed me off when I saw the mustang trunk scoop for $179.95 I got mine from a mustang place online for $61.00 w/shipping. That just made me loose a lot of respect for them. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||8
Z||000009||MontanaFiero||03-06-2001||03:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnnyK:<BR><B>Make you go faster? I don't understand how running the water pump and alternator faster will make your car faster? Wouldn't it just produce more power from the alternator and faster coolant flow? (Well, i know it would, I just want to hear your take on this story.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  The pullet has nothing to do with the speed of the car. All it does is drive the accessories.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What Standard was saying is that changing the pulley ratio by putting on a smaller pulley makes it <I>easier</I> for the motor to turn the alternator and water pump. It does <I>not</I> make the alt/water pump turn faster, it slows them down-- but that does <I>not</I> have anything to do with making the engine go faster/slower. It's the fact that the motor doesn't have to <I>work as hard</I> to turn the accessories and can use the left over horsepower to "rev". That makes a "faster" motor.  Clearer?<BR><P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000010||JohnnyK||03-06-2001||05:56 PM||||Yeah, theoretically, but i'm sure there is no difference. Unless your pump and what not are seized up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||hugh||03-06-2001||08:30 PM||||Every little bit helps.I've had a small pulley on my GN for years and all I can see is the charging at an idle is not the best,but how much time does anyone spend idling? ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Black88GT||03-07-2001||01:34 AM||||Johnny there is a difference in performance.  Shaun proved that.||||reg||1
